From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: tag_qca_8021q: carry a frame sent on a bridged port
 in the bridge's VLAN

Under qca-8021q a port that joins a bridge swaps its own tag_8021q VLAN
for the bridge's, and the bridge sends through it with offload_fwd_mark,
in that VLAN, with the switch forwarding by its own FDB. A frame sent
on the port netdev itself still got the port's own VLAN, of which the
port is no longer a member, and the switch dropped it.

The software flowtable sends exactly such frames: once a flow is in it,
FLOW_OFFLOAD_XMIT_DIRECT hands the packets to the bridge port the FDB
names, bypassing the bridge. On an ASUS RT-AX89X with the QCA8337 under
qca-8021q, a client on lan7 could ping and resolve names, but every TCP
connection froze after the handshake and a few replies, while the same
flows worked with flow_offloading off, and from the ports of the SoC's
own switch.

Carry such a frame in the bridge's VLAN: the tag_8021q bridge VID for a
VLAN-unaware bridge, the port's PVID for a VLAN-aware one, or the VLAN
tag it already wears. The switch then delivers it by its FDB, which also
puts it on the right port when the bridge learnt the station on another
one (imprecise RX).

Only unicast moves: the flowtable sends nothing else this way, and a
broadcast or multicast frame sent on the port itself (arping -I lan7, a
packet socket bound to the port) would otherwise be flooded to every
port of the bridge. Those keep the port's VLAN, as before.

Link: https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt/pull/26141#issuecomment-6444417923
Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/net/dsa/tag_qca_8021q.c
+++ b/net/dsa/tag_qca_8021q.c
@@ -8,6 +8,8 @@
  * plain 802.1Q, which any MAC - and any hardware in front of it - parses.
  */
 #include <linux/dsa/8021q.h>
+#include <linux/etherdevice.h>
+#include <linux/if_bridge.h>
 #include <linux/if_vlan.h>
 #include <net/dsa.h>
 
@@ -76,6 +78,26 @@ static struct sk_buff *qca_8021q_xmit(st
 			return skb;
 
 		tx_vid = dsa_tag_8021q_bridge_vid(bridge_num);
+	} else if (dp->bridge && dp->bridge->tx_fwd_offload &&
+		   is_unicast_ether_addr(eth_hdr(skb)->h_dest)) {
+		struct net_device *br = dsa_port_bridge_dev_get(dp);
+		u16 pvid;
+
+		/* A unicast frame sent on a bridged port itself, not by the
+		 * bridge: the flowtable transmits its flows this way
+		 * (FLOW_OFFLOAD_XMIT_DIRECT). The port's own VLAN went with
+		 * bridging, so carry the frame in the bridge's VLAN instead,
+		 * where the switch delivers it by its own FDB - to the right
+		 * port even when the bridge learnt the station on another one
+		 * (imprecise RX). Broadcast and multicast keep the port's VLAN.
+		 */
+		if (!br_vlan_enabled(br))
+			tx_vid = dsa_tag_8021q_bridge_vid(dsa_port_bridge_num_get(dp));
+		else if (skb_vlan_tag_present(skb) ||
+			 skb->protocol == htons(ETH_P_8021Q))
+			return skb;
+		else if (!br_vlan_get_pvid_rcu(netdev, &pvid) && pvid)
+			tx_vid = pvid;
 	}
 
 	/* An 802.1Q upper of the port (wan.35 for a PPPoE ISP, wan.99 next
